From 6963b951723867ffd113746c132417dd94b6ddea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lane <lane@local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 01:04:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 17/29] graph: wire iteration groups and the N-loop into
 GraphExecutor::execute()

Task 3 of 3. Design first, per instruction: docs/port-engineering/
iteration-group-resource-lifetime.md, citing renderer.js/iteration.js at
the pinned authority revision (61aa869). Summary of the design: an
iterated group's step-scoped and group-shared resources stay OUT of
ResourceArena entirely, mirroring the JS authority's own
state.resources/groupResources (never merged into its own `surfaces`
map either) -- so nothing in a group's N-iteration run can be bound by
name from outside the run that owns it. Only the group's final surface
(an independent clone) crosses into the arena, exactly like a
non-iterated step's own output already does. No new ResourceLifetime
enumerator, no new ResourceArena method.

executor.cpp: compute_iteration_groups now partitions every chain
before execution. A non-iterated group is always exactly one step
(iteration.hpp's own invariant) and runs through the EXACT SAME code
that already existed, untouched, byte-for-byte -- this is the
zero-overhead fast path every currently-admitted effect takes. An
iterated group instead runs through the new run_iterated_group: it
resolves N (iteration::resolve_iteration_count), seeds global_accum for
a loop region, builds one GroupStepState per step (declared scratch +
refreshed inputTex/surface-parameter routes, persisting across all N
iterations; a selfTex/feedback surface allocated once and updated by a
raw byte copy at the end of every iteration, never stored under a name
either publish path could replace), and runs the whole pass graph N
times with a per-iteration ExecutionInputs (frame=i,
delta_time=kIterationDeltaTime,
time=wrap01(inputs.time-(N-1-i)*kIterationDeltaTime)) threaded through
BindingMaterializationContext -- every existing authenticated
materialization helper (preflight_pass_abi,
materialize_uniform_bindings, materialize_sampler_bindings,
apply_classic_noisedeck_palette_override, bind_factory_route,
materialize_scatter_bindings, scatter_pass_from_definition, run_pass,
quantize_texture) is reused unchanged, only the route-lookup callback
and per-iteration inputs differ. Both real pass shapes (ordinary single
output, and scatter) are wired; MRT needs no branch here either, since
validate_pass_output_abi already refuses any non-single-output pass,
iterated or not, before execute()'s main loop reaches it.

ResourceArena::find/insert are private (friend GraphExecutor only) --
a lambda inside execute() inherits that access, a free function does
not. Every new helper is a free function, so it only ever reads the
arena through the public require() (wrapped to return nullptr instead
of throwing); the two spots that must publish a finished surface
(zero_iteration_group_output, run_iterated_group's final output)
return it to execute() instead, which performs the actual arena.insert
itself. noisemaker::Surface has no default constructor, so
std::unordered_map<std::string, Surface>::operator[] cannot be used --
store_step_resource() mirrors GroupResourceMap::store's own
find-then-emplace-or-move-assign shape.

Zero behavior change for every currently-admitted effect:
compute_iteration_groups only ever opens an iterated group when the
owning step's EffectDefinition::iterated is true, or it opens/joins a
loop region or particle group -- and every one of the 24 catalog
entries with iterated=true, plus render/pointsEmit and
render/loopBegin/loopEnd, refuses today with missing_backend_program
(confirmed by a full-catalog sweep). No admitted chain can ever produce
an iterated group; the new path is reachable only from a hand-built
synthetic plan.

Native tests (tests/test_graph_iterated_execution.cpp, new): drives
synthetic multi-step plans through the REAL executor using existing
admitted kernels (synth/perlin, filter/invert, filter/highPass) as step
bodies -- the same "mutate a DSL-compiled plan's
EffectDefinition::iterated/loop_role, then reauthenticate" technique
test_graph_features.cpp already uses for admission-tampering tests,
never a hand-forged admission. Covers: N-iteration count (pass_count
scales with N for N=1/3/7), the per-iteration time/frame/deltaTime
formula matched exactly against a direct non-iterated call computed
with iteration::wrap01/kIterationDeltaTime, N=0 (clones/clears with no
passes run), and a real 3-effect chain (perlin() ordinary + invert()/
highPass() marked as a loop region) proving compute_iteration_groups
recognizes a multi-step region and run_iterated_group dispatches all of
it correctly, byte-exact against a direct one-shot equivalent. Explicit,
documented scope limit: particle-state/global_accum ROUTING, persistent
scratch state, and selfTex/feedback cannot be exercised through a real
kernel, because every admitted program's sampler/output ABI is
SHA-256-pinned against its real GLSL route names -- renaming one to
"global_xyz"/"selfTex" for a test would fail authentication, not
exercise the code differently. Those mechanics have no real kernel to
drive them through yet; that is exactly the Family B/C/D/E gap this
lane remains blocked on.

